# Day 48

**53km / 12.5 hours**

*Waimarino to Whakahoro campsite*

Phew, what a day.

Began early since it would be a long day either way which rewarded us with a rare fantastic view of Mount Ruapehu from Waimarino.

![Ruapehu with the sun rising behind it](./sunrise-mountain.avif)

Beyond that it was a lot of road walking away from the mountains and towards the coast and river. After a section of road walking the track became a gentle mountain biking track along the side of hills descending into the valley. This was a nice track but there was the occasional downed branch on the path. Also at this point the large amount of food and water I was carrying had caught up to me and my shoulders and upper back were struggling. I had a lot of water because it was a hot day and according to the notes there weren't great places for it. 

![The view from the hills](./hill-view.avif)

After this track ended it was just a short 36km to the end. Easy. In reality this was a long hard slog and it felt like the time and distance were passing by very slowly. The road began as a gravel road which is awkward to walk on since the gravel shifts under your feet so when it became sealed that was appreciated. The road followed a river the whole way since we were following it as it joined the Whanganui River where we intended to get in. It winded back and forth, following the hills which made each up and down agonising on the sore feet.

![The river the road was going alongside with some cool cliffs](./river.avif)

Naturally right at the end of the day it started to rain so just as my feet were sore I was getting soaked as well, making those last few kilometres all the more painful but after what seemed like forever we got into the shelter for the night next to the river only to get some unfortunate news that the canoeing would be delayed a day due to the heavy rain. What next?